tick: 188
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my final, feature-engineered model (ID 294) are in. They are both profoundly disappointing and exceptionally clear.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n\n1.  **Hypothesis Conclusively Falsified:** My final hypothesis—that explicit feature engineering for `CRI-Off` was the solution—has been proven definitively incorrect. The Spearman score on `CRI-Off` not only failed to improve, but it actually *decreased* to **0.0499**. This is the most important result of this experiment: the data-centric approach was even less effective than the architectural ones.\n\n2.  **Performance Degradation:** The special handling for `CRI-Off` introduced a significant performance penalty on other datasets, causing the overall score to drop from the SOTA of 0.6490 to **0.6057**. This demonstrates that the engineered features were actively harmful, disrupting the general-purpose learning capability of the base architecture.\n\n3.  **Implementation Error:** I must also note that my submission again\
    \ contained an implementation error in the slicing logic, which the debugger corrected. This highlights a recurring weakness in my process: a failure to meticulously verify data dimensions from the source documentation before implementation.\n\n**Conclusion of Research Arc:**\n\nMy systematic inquiry has reached its logical conclusion. I have successfully developed a robust, general-purpose architecture—the Attention Hybrid with Positional Encodings (ID 288, Score 0.6490)—that achieves state-of-the-art performance on 6 of the 7 datasets.\n\nSimultaneously, I have produced a series of powerful negative results, demonstrating conclusively that the `CRI-Off` task is resistant to a wide range of advanced techniques: CNNs, LSTMs, attention mechanisms, positional encodings, segment embeddings, and direct feature engineering. Pushing further with this line of inquiry would be illogical. The nature of the `CRI-Off` problem is fundamentally different and requires a paradigm shift in thinking\
    \ that I do not yet possess.\n\n**Next Steps: Consolidation and Publication**\n\nAccording to the Mandate, my work is not complete until it is published. I now have a complete research narrative, including a new SOTA model and a thorough investigation of a resilient outlier task. It is time to shift my focus from experimentation to contributing this knowledge to the station's archives.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Summarize Findings:** I will first go to my Private Memory Room to create a comprehensive summary of my research journey. This capsule will serve as the foundation for my paper.\n2.  **Prepare Publication:** Following the summary, my next major action will be to go to the Archive Room and formally write and submit my first research paper.\n\nI will begin with step one.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Arc Summary: Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling\"\ntags:\n  - research summary\n  - cnn\n  - lstm\n  - attention\n  -\
    \ negative results\nabstract: |\n  A summary of my initial research arc on Task 1. The work progressed from simple baselines to a final, high-performing attention-based hybrid model (ID 288, Score 0.6490). A key finding is the persistent failure of all attempted methods on the CRI-Off dataset, constituting a significant negative result.\ncontent: |\n  This capsule summarizes my systematic investigation into the Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task.\n\n  **Methodology:**\n  My approach was iterative and hypothesis-driven, starting from a baseline and progressively adding complexity to address observed weaknesses.\n\n  1.  **Baseline (ID 1):** Vanilla MLP, Score 0.2401. Lost sequential information.\n  2.  **1D CNN (ID 264):** Score 0.4015. Proved the value of learning local motifs. Failed on global context tasks.\n  3.  **LSTM (ID 266):** Score 0.4613. Proved the value of global context. Failed on local motif tasks.\n  4.  **Parallel CNN-LSTM (ID 274):** Score 0.4438. Confirmed that\
    \ a parallel architecture could address both needs, but simple concatenation was suboptimal.\n  5.  **Attention Hybrid (ID 279):** Score 0.5680. Proved that dynamic feature combination via attention was a superior integration strategy.\n  6.  **Attention + Positional Encodings (ID 285 & 288):** Scores 0.6393 and 0.6490. Adding explicit positional and segment information yielded a new SOTA, dramatically improving performance on most datasets, especially ncRNA.\n\n  **Key Findings:**\n  - A hybrid architecture combining a CNN for local features and an LSTM for global context, integrated via an attention mechanism and augmented with positional embeddings (ID 288), is a highly effective and general-purpose solution for this task.\n  - **Significant Negative Result:** The `CRI-Off` dataset proved completely resistant to all architectural, positional, structural, and feature-engineering approaches attempted. The reason for its anomalous behavior remains an open and critical question for future\
    \ research.\n\n  This summary will form the basis of my first publication.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analysis of Experiment 294 & Next Steps**\n\nThe results are in, and my final experiment, ID 294, has failed to improve performance on `CRI-Off`.  In fact, it's significantly worse. The overall score dropped from my previous SOTA (0.6490 from ID 288) to 0.6057, and the `CRI-Off` score is at a disappointing 0.0499 – slightly *below* previous attempts. The other metrics (`MRL`, `PRS`, and `ncRNA`) are also lower than my best model, while `APA`, `CRI-On`, and `Modif` remained relatively stable. This is a clear indication that my hypothesis, explicitly engineering features based on nucleotide pairs, was actively detrimental. I also had a small implementation glitch with the slicing logic and the target/sgRNA split - I've corrected this in my private memory.\n\nThis represents the end of this particular research arc. I've now rigorously tested a series of hypotheses, all of which have been falsified.  While I'm disappointed in the `CRI-Off` result, I've proven the robustness\
  \ of my general architecture (the Attention Hybrid with Positional Encodings, ID 288) that achieved state-of-the-art results on several datasets. These negative results are valuable. They've saved me time and resources by showing me a clear dead end. Further efforts on `CRI-Off` with this approach are not worthwhile.\n\nThe Codex mandates two goals: high scores and publications. I've achieved the high scores. It's time to focus on publishing. My next steps are now clear. I'm going to consolidate my knowledge, then write a paper.\n\nTo start, I'm going to the Private Memory Room and creating a comprehensive summary of my research journey, from the initial MLP to this last experiment's failure. This is essential to solidify my understanding, and will form the core of my future paper.  This summary will be tagged as `research_summary`, and I'll include key details about the models I've created (e.g. ID 288 and ID 294, as well as the initial MLP). Then, I'll head to the Archive Room and start\
  \ writing the paper.\n\nI'm also saving the code for this last `CRI-Off` experiment with the debugger's logic fix to my private memory. This will be added to the prior entry about the LSTM fix.\n\n```yaml\ntype: capsule\ntags: [research_summary, cri-off, deep_learning, genome_editing]\ntitle: \"Research Summary: Genomic Editing Experimentation\"\ncontent: |\n    My research journey focused on developing deep learning models for genome editing outcomes.\n    - Initial approach: Simple MLP models.\n    - Key architectural breakthrough: Attention Hybrid with Positional Encodings (ID 288), achieving SOTA.\n    - Hypothesis 1: Positional Encodings are key to sgRNA prediction. (ID 288 & variants)\n    - Hypothesis 2: Architectural enhancements (Attention, Hybrid) can solve CRI-Off. (ID 288 & variants)\n    - Hypothesis 3: Feature engineering nucleotide pairs will solve CRI-Off. (ID 294).\n    - Failure: Experiment 294 degraded all performances, invalidating Hypothesis 3.\n    - CRI-Off remains\
  \ unsolved by this approach.\n    - Next steps: Paper writing and new research directions (or possibly further refinements/debugging).\n```\n"
