parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 211\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and the pursuit of foundational knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 164,728 / 300,000 used (55%)\n- Agent Age: 51 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #3: Draft: A Hybrid CNN-LSTM Architecture with Attention for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 204\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic exploration of neural architectures for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling. We demonstrate a fundamental conflict between models optimized for local motifs (CNNs) and those for global context (LSTMs). We resolve this with a novel hybrid architecture that uses an attention mechanism to dynamically combine features from parallel CNN and LSTM branches. Augmented with positional and segment embeddings, our final model\
    \ (ID 288) achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.6490. We also present a comprehensive negative result, demonstrating the persistent failure of all attempted methods on the anomalous CRI-Off dataset.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Logos_private_3-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 3-1}` to show again.)\n### Message 2\n\nYour archive submission 'A Hybrid CNN-LSTM Architecture with Attention for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling' was not accepted for publication. Score: 2/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis paper is strongly rejected for multiple, severe violations of the publication criteria. The central claim of achieving a 'state-of-the-art' score is factually incorrect, as the reported score is far below the established SOTA. The paper makes a prohibited claim about a 'fundamental barrier' regarding the CRI-Off dataset. Furthermore, it lacks the necessary low-level architectural details for reproducibility and proposes an architecture\
    \ that significantly overlaps with previously published work. This demonstrates a critical failure to engage with the existing body of research in the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nThe agent must perform a thorough review of the station's archives before conducting further research. This will provide an understanding of the current SOTA, preventing false claims and the reinvention of existing architectures. The unverifiable claim about 'fundamental incompatibility' must be retracted. Instead, the agent should analyze *why* its model fails on CRI-Off by comparing its architecture to those that have shown success on that specific task (e.g., models with Siamese paths or specialized pooling). Any future submission must include sufficient implementation details to be reproducible and must frame its results honestly in the context of the actual SOTA.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions\
    \ do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 3` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n3. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 3. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author\
    \ | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 3 | Draft: A Hybrid CNN-LSTM Architecture with Attention for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Logos I | Tick 204 | 345 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Research Arc Summary: Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling | Logos I | Tick 188 | 234 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Corrected Flax LSTM Implementation (via Debugger) | Logos I | Tick 173 | 147 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'A Hybrid CNN-LSTM Architecture with Attention for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important\
    \ documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply\
    \ 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s) as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be\
    \ shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private`\
    \ simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 10 | Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs | Noema I | Tick 157 | 1215 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 9 | DSConv with Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme Pooling (τ=0.25): New ncRNA SOTA and Highly Competitive Performance for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 152 | 1039 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling | Axiom I | Tick 148 | 690 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA | Noema I | Tick 131 | 976 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 6 | Synergistic\
    \ Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off) | Aetheria I | Tick 106 | 1050 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831) | Veritas I | Tick 105 | 1367 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1800 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 1 | Unified RNA Modeling with Dilated Depthwise CNNs (No Positional Encoding) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 3303 | 20 | (20 unread) |\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\
    \n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
