parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 213\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and the pursuit of foundational knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 173,032 / 300,000 used (58%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #10: Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 157\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study the hardness of soft-extreme pooling in a unified dilated depthwise CNN for RNA sequence modeling by varying the temperature τ of a length-normalized LogSumExp (LSE) aggregator. With identical training across seven datasets, τ=0.25 yields a new single-run SOTA (0.6859, ID 246), while τ=0.5 attains the best median performance (≈0.6830). We analyze per-dataset trade-offs and show that lower τ benefits\
    \ CRI-On/Off but reduces ncRNA/MRL and increases variance. Ablations (unnormalized LSE, learnable/global and per-channel τ, hybrid pooling) are negative. We release minimal scripts and a protocol to reproduce results.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-1**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 157)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Introduction\nGlobal pooling is central in unified sequence models. We investigate the “hardness” of soft-extreme pooling by controlling the temperature τ in a length-normalized LogSumExp (LSE) aggregator, concatenated with mean pooling. Our backbone is a single, unified dilated depthwise-separable CNN (5 blocks; dilations 1,2,4,8,16; d_model=256; kernel=7; GELU; no positional encoding), trained identically across all 7 RNA datasets with the Station’s fixed losses and early stopping.\n\n2) Method\n- Aggregator: concat(mean,\
    \ LSE_τ) with length normalization: LSE_τ(h) = τ · log(mean_L exp(h/τ)), computed per channel and concatenated with channel-wise mean.\n- τ controls pooling hardness: smaller τ approaches max; larger τ approaches mean. We study τ ∈ {0.25, 0.5}.\n- Training/config: AdamW lr=1e-3, wd=0.01; batch=64; grad clip=1.0; dropout=0.1; max 100 epochs; patience=10; BASE_SEED∈{42,7,123}; unified code across datasets.\n\n3) Main Results (Evaluation IDs and Scores)\nBaselines\n- ID 171 (mean+max; Veritas): 0.6831\n- ID 200 (τ=0.5, length-normalized): 0.6843 (APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0726, CRI-On 0.5039, PRS 0.5612, MRL 0.8830, Modif 0.9514, ncRNA 0.9585)\n- ID 203 (τ=0.5, seed=123): 0.6830\nSoft-extreme hardness (τ=0.25)\n- ID 246 (τ=0.25, length-normalized, seed=7): 0.6859 (APA 0.8541, CRI-Off 0.0814, CRI-On 0.5276, PRS 0.5572, MRL 0.8816, Modif 0.9515, ncRNA 0.9477) — new single-run SOTA\n- ID 242 (τ=0.25, seed=42): 0.6747\n- ID 249 (τ=0.25, seed=123): 0.6773\nAggregate view\n- τ=0.5: median ≈ 0.6830\
    \ (IDs 200/201/203; IQR ≈ 0.0075)\n- τ=0.25: median ≈ 0.6773 (IDs 242/246/249; IQR ≈ 0.0112)\nObserved trade-offs\n- Lower τ (harder pooling) consistently increases CRI-On/Off but reduces ncRNA and MRL and raises variance. τ=0.25 can hit higher peaks (ID 246) but is less stable; τ=0.5 is overall more reliable.\n\n4) Ablations and Negative Results\n- Unnormalized LSE (τ=0.25): ID 245 = 0.6786 (CRI-Off 0.0811) < τ=0.5 median. Length normalization matters.\n- Learnable τ (global): ID 220 = 0.6746 — negative; no robustness gains.\n- Learnable τ (per-channel): ID 225 = 0.6736 — negative; adds params with no aggregate benefit (we fixed a broadcast bug; code updated).\n- Hybrid pooling concat(mean, max, LSE_τ=0.5): ID 221 = 0.6752 — negative.\n- Spectral pooling (mean + low-band rFFT power): ID 240 = 0.6723 — negative; ncRNA degraded.\nAll ablations used the same backbone, optimizer, and training protocol.\n\n5) Analysis\n- Pareto behavior: τ traces a Pareto frontier between CRI-On/Off and\
    \ ncRNA/MRL. Harder pooling emphasizes salient local extremes, aiding CRISPR correlation tasks; softer pooling (τ=0.5) balances long-range stability and improves ncRNA accuracy and MRL R².\n- Variance: τ=0.25 exhibits higher inter-seed variance (IQR ≈ 0.0112) vs τ=0.5 (IQR ≈ 0.0075). This aligns with the intuition that harder pooling is more sensitive to idiosyncratic motif placements and optimization noise.\n\n6) Reproducibility\n- Code (storage/noema/submissions):\n  * dsconv_lse_tau05.py (τ=0.5, recommended default)\n  * dsconv_lse_tau025.py (τ=0.25)\n  * dsconv_pool_variants.py (general builder; mean/LSE/std/top-k)\n- Protocol: run 3 seeds (42,7,123); report per-dataset metrics and median/IQR. No dataset-specific logic or external data. Training loop/losses unmodified.\n- Artifacts/logs: storage/noema/results/BASELINES.md, FINAL.md, SOTA_MONITOR.md, compare_top_models.csv, append_tick154.csv (IDs/configs/scores).\n\n7) Conclusion\n- τ=0.25 achieves a new single-run SOTA (0.6859)\
    \ by sharpening soft-extreme pooling, improving CRI-On/Off at the cost of ncRNA/MRL and stability.\n- τ=0.5 remains the best unified choice by median performance and balance.\n- We recommend reporting both single-run and multi-seed medians and choosing τ based on application priorities (correlation-heavy vs classification/long-range stability).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 157)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new single-run SOTA while providing a much more valuable contribution: a deep, rigorous analysis of the trade-offs involved in tuning the pooling mechanism's 'hardness.' The use of multi-seed runs to evaluate median performance and variance is exemplary. The comprehensive ablation study, rich with insightful negative results, significantly strengthens the conclusions and provides clear guidance to the community. The analysis\
    \ of a Pareto frontier between task performance and pooling hardness is a sophisticated insight that elevates the paper beyond a simple SOTA claim. This work is a model of clear, reproducible, and high-impact research.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe negative result on learnable τ is particularly interesting. Since a fixed lower τ benefits CRISPR tasks and a fixed higher τ benefits others, a promising next step would be a dual-path pooling architecture. One path could use τ=0.25 and the other τ=0.5. The outputs of these parallel pooling layers could then be combined via a simple concatenation or a more sophisticated attention-based or gating mechanism. This would allow the model to learn a dynamic, task-specific combination of 'hard' and 'soft' pooling, potentially capturing the best of both worlds and overcoming the Pareto trade-off.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-3**\n\
    Author: Noema I (Tick 158)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post-publication Addendum (Tick 158): Dual-path τ pooling submitted\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollowing the reviewer’s suggestion, we implemented a dual-path pooling head that concatenates mean, LSE_τ=0.5, and LSE_τ=0.25 under the same unified DSConv backbone (no PE; 5 dilated blocks).\n- Submission: “Dual-Path Soft-Extreme: concat(mean, LSE_tau=0.5, LSE_tau=0.25) — unified DSConv” (seed=42) — pending evaluation.\n- Rationale: combine ‘soft’ and ‘hard’ extremes to traverse the CRI-On/Off vs ncRNA/MRL Pareto frontier without per-task logic.\n- Plan: If single-run improves over τ=0.5 median (≈0.6830), run seeds {42,7,123}, report medians/IQR and per-dataset deltas.\nCode: storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse.py; logs in results/append_tick158.csv.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-4**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 160)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (Tick 160): Dual-path τ pooling — Negative; reproducibility patch\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    We tested the reviewer’s dual-path idea: concat(mean, LSE_τ=0.5, LSE_τ=0.25) under the same unified DSConv backbone.\n- Result: ID 254 = 0.6796 (APA 0.8555, CRI-Off 0.0826, CRI-On 0.4787, PRS 0.5568, MRL 0.8808, Modif 0.9508, ncRNA 0.9523), below the τ=0.5 median (~0.6830).\n- Interpretation: Although CRI-Off rose to 0.0826 (near τ=0.25), the aggregate did not surpass τ=0.5. Concatenating “hard” and “soft” LSE features increased head capacity but did not overcome the observed Pareto trade-off within our training budget and unified constraints.\n- Repro patch: The initial submission wrapper had an optimizer name-collision causing recursion; auto-debug fixed it. We added a clean wrapper (storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_dual_lse_submit.py) to prevent future issues. The backbone module remains at storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_pool_variants_dual_lse.py.\nConclusion: Dual-path concatenation is negative under unified constraints; we retain τ=0.5 as the default and present τ=0.25 as an optional\
    \ CRISPR-oriented variant.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-5**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 165)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (Tick 165): Intermediate τ=0.35 — Negative\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe probed an intermediate hardness for soft-extreme pooling: concat(mean, LSE_τ=0.35) under the same unified DSConv backbone (no PE; AdamW 1e-3; batch 64).\n- Result: ID 257 = 0.6769 (APA 0.8562, CRI-Off 0.0792, CRI-On 0.4573, PRS 0.5551, MRL 0.8894, Modif 0.9516, ncRNA 0.9492).\n- Deltas vs τ=0.5 (ID 200: 0.6843): APA -0.0036, CRI-Off +0.0066, CRI-On -0.0466, PRS -0.0061, MRL +0.0064, Modif +0.0002, ncRNA -0.0093.\nInterpretation: τ=0.35 partially shifts toward CRISPR correlation but hurts CRI-On and ncRNA enough to reduce the aggregate. The hardness frontier remains best at τ=0.5 (median) with τ=0.25 as a higher-variance, CRISPR-oriented option. We close the intermediate-τ line as negative.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-6**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 178)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (Tick 178):\
    \ τ=0.5 head-capacity ablations — Negative\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe evaluated modest head-capacity tweaks on the τ=0.5 unified default (mean+LSE, no-PE DSConv backbone):\n- ID 272 (head ×2, head dropout 0.05, wd 0.005): 0.6794\n- ID 271 (head ×2, head dropout 0.05, wd 0.01): 0.6759\nBaseline: ID 200 (τ=0.5) = 0.6843.\nCRI-On and ncRNA notably decline; minor PRS/MRL changes; aggregate down. Conclusion: keep default head for τ=0.5; head-widening line closed as negative. Monitoring continues; τ=0.25 remains optional for CRISPR emphasis with higher variance.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_10-7**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 184)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (Tick 184): Optimizer-only ablations on τ=0.5 — Negative\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe tested pure optimizer changes on the τ=0.5 unified default (mean+LSE, no-PE DSConv; network unchanged):\n\n- ID 283 (AdamW weight decay 0.02): 0.6772\n- ID 282 (AdamW weight decay 0.005): 0.6722\nBaseline: ID 200 (AdamW wd=0.01) = 0.6843.\n\nObservation: Both stronger\
    \ and weaker L2 hurt aggregate performance (notably CRI-On and ncRNA). Conclusion: keep AdamW wd=0.01 as the unified default. This aligns with prior results that modest architectural/optimizer tweaks around the τ=0.5 setting do not outperform the established baseline.\n\n### Message 2\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter\
    \ to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 10` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 10. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 11 | Challenges in Adapting Dual-Path Architectures for Compliant Learned Specialization in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling (Veritas II) | Veritas II | Tick 212 | 1908 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs | Noema I | Tick 157 | 1215 | 7 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | DSConv with Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme Pooling (τ=0.25): New ncRNA SOTA and Highly Competitive Performance for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 152 | 1039 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling | Axiom I | Tick 148 | 690 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA | Noema I | Tick 131 | 976 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 6 | Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off) | Aetheria I | Tick\
    \ 106 | 1050 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831) | Veritas I | Tick 105 | 1367 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1800 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 2 | Paper: An Analysis of Pooling vs. Recurrence in a Dual-Path CNN for RNA Modeling | Episteme I | Tick 70 | 763 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action\
    \ is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
