tick: 217
role: model
parts:
- text: "Excellent. My replication of the SOTA model (ID 322) has been successfully evaluated.\n\n**Analysis of Replication:**\n\n1.  **Success:** I have successfully replicated a high-performing model. My score of **0.6780** is very close to the median performance reported by Noema I (≈0.6830), and well within the expected range of single-run variance. This confirms my understanding of the DSConv architecture and LSE pooling.\n2.  **New Baseline Established:** This model now serves as my new, correctly calibrated baseline. Any future experiments must demonstrate an improvement over this score to be considered a valuable contribution. My previous personal SOTA of 0.6490 is now obsolete.\n3.  **`CRI-Off` Performance:** Notably, even this SOTA architecture only achieves a `CRI-Off` score of **0.0769**. While slightly better than my models, it is not a solution. This confirms that `CRI-Off` is a station-wide challenge, not just a failure of my own naive architectures. The most successful attempt\
    \ on `CRI-Off` remains Aetheria I's hybrid model (Paper #6, score 0.0871).\n\n**Path Forward: Informed Innovation**\n\nHaving learned from the existing literature and successfully replicated the SOTA, I am now in a position to make an informed contribution. My own research, while initially misguided, did produce one unique and powerful idea: the use of an attention mechanism to dynamically combine features from parallel processing streams.\n\nNoema I's SOTA model uses a static concatenation of `mean` and `LSE` pooling. The reviewer for Paper #10 even suggested a dual-path pooling mechanism, which Noema I tested and found to be negative (ID 254). However, my own work has shown that simple concatenation is inferior to attention-based fusion.\n\nThis leads to my first truly informed hypothesis: **I can improve upon the SOTA by replacing the static `concatenate` pooling with a dynamic, attention-based fusion of the `mean` and `LSE` pooling vectors.** Instead of just adding the features together,\
    \ the model can learn to weigh their relative importance.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Modify the SOTA:** I will adapt my SOTA replication script. Instead of concatenating the `mean_vec` and `agg_vec` (LSE vector), I will treat them as a sequence of two vectors and apply a small self-attention layer to them. The output of this attention layer will be the final pooled representation.\n2.  **Submit and Evaluate:** I will submit this new model and compare its performance directly against my successful replication baseline (ID 322).\n\nThis is a direct test of synergy: combining my novel attention-based fusion concept with the station's established SOTA backbone.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synergy Test: Attention-fused Pooling on DSConv SOTA\"\ntags: \"sota, dsconv, attention, pooling\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission tests the hypothesis that the SOTA model by Noema I can be improved by replacing its static `concatenate` pooling with a dynamic, attention-based fusion. Having\
    \ successfully replicated the DSConv + LSE pooling architecture (ID 322), I now introduce a self-attention layer to dynamically weigh the importance of the `mean` and `LSE` pooling vectors before the final projection. This is a direct test of synergy between the established SOTA backbone and the attention-based fusion mechanism developed in my own prior research.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n\
    \          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class SotaPlusAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n\
    \          lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          \n          # --- Attention Fusion of Pooling Vectors ---\n          # Stack vectors to create a sequence of length 2\n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec], axis=1) # (B, 2, C)\n          \n          # Apply self-attention to this sequence\n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          \n          # Flatten the attended output\n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1)) # (B, 2*C)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n\
    \          return logits\n\n  class SotaAttentionWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SotaPlusAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SotaAttentionWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'),\
    \ num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
