tick: 222
role: model
parts:
- text: "My second paper submission has also been rejected. This time, the failure is not one of ignorance, but of insufficient rigor.\n\n**Analysis of Rejection:**\n\nThe reviewer's feedback is precise and actionable:\n1.  **Insufficient Experimental Evidence:** My paper was based on only three core experiments (replication, attention-fusion τ=0.5, attention-fusion τ=0.25). The station requires a minimum of five to establish a robust claim. This is a clear procedural failure on my part.\n2.  **Lack of Reproducibility:** I failed again to include specific implementation details in my paper's text. While the code was submitted, the paper itself must be self-contained and provide enough information for others to understand and replicate the core idea.\n\n**Corrective Action Plan:**\n\nI will address these two points directly. My core idea of attention-fused pooling is promising, but I must substantiate it with more evidence. The reviewer provided excellent suggestions.\n\n1.  **Conduct Additional\
    \ Experiments:** I need two more experiments to meet the 5-experiment threshold. I will perform the following ablations on my attention-fused pooling model:\n    *   **Experiment 4: Varying Attention Heads:** I will test the effect of changing the number of attention heads (e.g., from 4 to 2) to see how it impacts performance. This tests the sensitivity of my mechanism to its own hyperparameters.\n    *   **Experiment 5: Testing a Different Pooling Combination:** I will replace the `LSE` pooling vector with a simple `max` pooling vector, creating a `mean + max` combination fused by attention. This will test the generality of my fusion mechanism beyond LSE pooling.\n2.  **Revise and Resubmit Paper:** After these experiments are complete, I will have a set of five core results. I will then rewrite my paper to include all five experiments, along with a dedicated section detailing the implementation of the attention-fused pooling layer (number of heads, dimensions, etc.) to ensure full reproducibility.\n\
    \nI will begin immediately with the first new experiment: testing with fewer attention heads. I must first return to the Research Counter.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: Attention-fused Pooling with 2 Heads\"\ntags: \"ablation, attention, pooling, dsconv\"\nabstract: |\n  This is an ablation study for my paper on attention-fused pooling. My best model (ID 325) used 4 attention heads. This experiment tests the sensitivity of the architecture by reducing the number of heads to 2. The goal is to provide additional experimental evidence required for publication and to understand the impact of this hyperparameter on my proposed fusion mechanism.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n  \
    \    @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class SotaPlusAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model,\
    \ name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          \n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec], axis=1)\n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1))\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n     \
    \     if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class SotaAttentionWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SotaPlusAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 2 # Reduced from 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SotaAttentionWrapper(\n\
    \          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
