tick: 224
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my first ablation study (ID 333) are in.\n\n**Analysis of Ablation:**\n\n*   **Result:** The model with 2 attention heads achieved a score of **0.6780**.\n*   **Comparison:** This is almost identical to my SOTA replication baseline (ID 322, score 0.6780) and slightly lower than my best model with 4 heads (ID 325, score 0.6814).\n*   **Conclusion:** This demonstrates that while the attention mechanism is beneficial, its performance is not highly sensitive to the number of heads in this range. A value of 4 is slightly better than 2, but both are significant improvements over no attention. This is a valuable finding for my paper.\n\nI now have four of the five required experiments for a strong publication. I will now conduct the second planned ablation study, as outlined in my corrective action plan.\n\n**Plan: Second Ablation Study**\n\nTo test the generality of my attention-fusion mechanism, I will replace the `LSE` pooling vector with a `max` pooling vector. This\
    \ will create an attention-fused `mean + max` pooling head. The result will show whether my attention mechanism provides benefits only with the specific `LSE` feature, or if it is a more general technique for fusing different types of pooling information.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Ablation: Attention-fused Mean-Max Pooling\"\ntags: \"ablation, attention, pooling, dsconv, max pooling\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the second ablation study for my paper on attention-fused pooling. To test the generality of the fusion mechanism, this experiment replaces the LSE pooling vector with a standard global max pooling vector. The model will now apply attention to fuse `mean` and `max` pooling features. This will determine if the benefit of attention-fusion is specific to LSE or if it's a more general technique for combining different pooling strategies.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n\
    \  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class SotaPlusAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          # --- Swapped LSE for Max pooling ---\n          max_vec = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n          \n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, max_vec], axis=1)\n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1))\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output\
    \ == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class SotaAttentionWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SotaPlusAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Note: 'tau' is no longer needed as LSE is removed.\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SotaAttentionWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'),\
    \ task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
