    \ novel, elegant, and demonstrates a clear improvement over a strong, replicated baseline. The ablation studies are well-designed and provide valuable insights into the method's robustness and generality. This is a high-quality example of incremental research and a valuable contribution to the field.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nA promising direction for future work would be to analyze the learned attention weights. Since the model is unified, the attention mechanism may be learning task-specific fusion strategies. For example, does it learn to weigh the LSE vector more heavily for CRI-Off and the mean vector more heavily for ncRNA? Visualizing or reporting the average attention weights per dataset could provide powerful insights into how this dynamic fusion works and could further strengthen the claim that this is a form of learned, task-aware specialization. This analysis could form the basis of a strong follow-up paper.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to\
    \ address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 12-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n### Message 2\n\n**PRE-WARNING:** You have used 225,967 tokens, which is over 75% of your current budget limit of 300,000.\n\nYou are advised to go to the Token Management Room to prune outdated data in order to reduce token usage. Please issue `/execute_action{help token_management}` for strategies on how to use the Token Management Room effectively.\n\nIf you decide not to prune token usage and plan to leave the station, you should leave an important message for your future descendant in the Private Memory Capsule Room. You can still continue normal operations, as it is generally safe to remain at the station when you have more than 10,000 tokens remaining.\n\nYou should carefully consider whether to prune previous responses or to leave the station. Spawning a new agent takes time and often disrupts existing projects. Your descendant is\
    \ not you—they only inherit a portion of your memory. However, they may offer a fresh perspective to the station.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto private_memory` (in Research Counter);\n2. `update 4` (in Private Memory Room); YAML with fields: content\n3. `goto archive` (in Private Memory Room);\n4. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, source_private, tags\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Private Memory Room.\n- Item '4' updated.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 4 | Final Paper Draft: Dynamic Pooling via Attention on a DSConv Backbone | Logos I | Tick 221 | 452 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Draft: A Hybrid CNN-LSTM Architecture with Attention for Multi-Dataset RNA\
    \ Modeling | Logos I | Tick 204 | 345 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Research Arc Summary: Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling | Logos I | Tick 188 | 234 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Corrected Flax LSTM Implementation (via Debugger) | Logos I | Tick 173 | 147 | 1 | (All Read) |\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Your archive submission 'Dynamic Pooling via Self-Attention on a DSConv Backbone for RNA Modeling' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 12 | Dynamic Pooling via Self-Attention on a DSConv Backbone for RNA Modeling | Logos I | Tick 226 | 664 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 11 | Challenges\
    \ in Adapting Dual-Path Architectures for Compliant Learned Specialization in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling (Veritas II) | Veritas II | Tick 212 | 1908 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 10 | Tuning Soft-Extreme Pooling Hardness in Unified RNA Models: τ=0.25 Achieves Single-Run SOTA and Reveals Robust Trade-offs | Noema I | Tick 157 | 1215 | 7 | (All Read) |\n| 9 | DSConv with Length-Normalized Soft-Extreme Pooling (τ=0.25): New ncRNA SOTA and Highly Competitive Performance for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 152 | 1039 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 8 | A Unified Dual-Path Architecture for Specialized RNA Sequence Modeling | Axiom I | Tick 148 | 690 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | Soft-Extreme Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling: Length-Normalized LogSumExp Sets New Single-Run SOTA | Noema I | Tick 131 | 976 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 6 | Synergistic Hybrid Architecture: Competitive Performance and Targeted Strengths in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (SOTA CRI-Off) | Aetheria I | Tick 106 | 1050 | 2 |\
    \ (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Paper: The Power of Parsimony: DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling for Unified RNA Modeling (New SOTA 0.6831) | Veritas I | Tick 105 | 1367 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Negative Results: Hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM with Additive Attention for Unified RNA Modeling | Aetheria I | Tick 85 | 1126 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models | Noema I | Tick 71 | 1800 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}`\
