- text: "I am in the Token Management Room. My token usage is critical, and I must prune my history to ensure continued operation.\n\nMy research can be divided into two main phases: my initial, isolated exploration (pre-tick 211) and my current, informed research phase (post-tick 211). The first phase, while a valuable learning experience for me, is now largely obsolete in its details. The raw code, intermediate hypotheses, and verbose logs from that period are no longer necessary for my current context, as my work is now grounded in the station's SOTA. I have already summarized this phase in my private memory.\n\nTherefore, I will prune the majority of my immature, pre-literature-review history. I will leave a few key inflection points unpruned for context, such as my ascension and the final SOTA model from that initial arc. The critical turning point was my first paper rejection at tick 211, which is protected. This is the perfect boundary.\n\n**Pruning Plan:**\n*   **Block 1 (Ticks 179-189):**\
    \ Prune the final experiments of my initial arc, which were incremental and ultimately superseded.\n*   **Block 2 (Ticks 181-189):** This is incorrect, I will combine the block.\n*   **Block 1 (Ticks 179-189):** My final naive experiments (positional encoding, segment embedding, feature engineering, positional weights) and the subsequent data analysis. These were all valuable negative results now summarized in my paper draft.\n*   **Block 2 (Ticks 192-210):** A period of experimentation (optimization, ablation, new ideas) and waiting for maturity. This block can be summarized concisely.\n\nLet me refine the plan. I will prune large, contiguous blocks.\n\n**Final Pruning Plan:**\n1.  **Block 1 (Ticks 179-210):** This block contains my final naive experiments, the discovery of my SOTA, the subsequent failed optimizations, my period of waiting, and my first paper rejection. The summary will note the development of my initial SOTA (ID 288, score 0.6490) and the crucial realization of my\
    \ ignorance upon reaching maturity. This entire block can be considered the \"end of the beginning.\"\n2.  **Block 2 (Ticks 168-177):** My first experiments (CNN, LSTM, Hybrids). These are well-documented in my research summary.\n\nThis is not optimal. The goal is to prune large blocks. I will prune everything from my first submission up to the tick before my first paper rejection.\n\n**Revised Final Pruning Plan:**\nI will prune the entire block from tick 168 to 210. This period covers my entire initial research arc, from my first analysis of the baseline to my first failed publication attempt. I have already summarized this entire process in my private memory (Capsule #2 and #3). The summary here can be concise, referencing those capsules.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"168-210\"\n    summary: |\n      This period covers my entire initial, isolated research arc (pre-literature review). I progressed from a baseline MLP to a CNN, LSTM, and\
    \ various hybrid architectures, culminating in a personal SOTA model (ID 288, score 0.6490) using an attention-based CNN-LSTM hybrid. This arc also included extensive negative results on the CRI-Off dataset. The period concluded upon reaching maturity (tick 210), after which I discovered my work was not station SOTA. The full research summary is in Private Capsule #2 and a paper draft in #3.\n```"
