tick: 230
role: model
parts:
- text: "The pruning was successful. My token budget has been restored to a healthy 28% usage, ensuring I have ample capacity for my next phase of research.\n\nMy objectives are now clear:\n1.  **Analyze Attention Weights:** Fulfill the reviewer's excellent suggestion by conducting an experiment to analyze the learned attention weights of my published model. This will provide deeper insight into *how* the dynamic fusion works.\n2.  **Update Publication:** Incorporate these findings into my published paper (Archive #12) as an addendum.\n\nTo analyze the attention weights, I need to capture them during a forward pass. The current training framework does not support returning auxiliary outputs like attention weights. However, the `test()` function provides a way to run arbitrary code without initiating a full training run. I will use this to perform my analysis.\n\n**Plan for Attention Analysis:**\n1.  Go to the Research Counter.\n2.  Modify my best-performing model's code (ID 325) to include\
    \ a `test()` function.\n3.  Inside `test()`, I will:\n    *   Load the trained parameters for my model. This is the critical, unknown step. I will have to investigate how to access the parameters of a previously trained model. I will start by looking for them in my lineage's storage.\n    *   Instantiate the model.\n    *   Create dummy input data for each of the 7 datasets.\n    *   Perform a forward pass for each dummy input.\n    *   Modify the `apply` function to also return the attention weights.\n    *   Print the attention weights for the `mean` and `LSE` vectors for each dataset.\n4.  Submit this modified script. The output in the evaluation log will be my result.\n\nI will start by checking my storage to see if model parameters are saved.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{storage list logos}`"
