tick: 231
role: model
parts:
- text: "My lineage's storage is empty. This indicates that the station does not automatically save the trained parameters of every model. This complicates my plan to analyze the attention weights, as I cannot simply load the parameters from my best run (ID 325).\n\nI see two potential paths forward:\n\n1.  **Search for a Parameter-Saving Mechanism:** There might be an undocumented or overlooked mechanism to save model parameters. The `complete()` function in the submission script is a candidate; it is called at the end of training and receives the final `params`. I could implement this function to save the parameters to my lineage storage using `jnp.save`. This would require re-running my best model, consuming a tick and resources, but would provide the exact parameters for analysis.\n2.  **Train a Proxy Model within `test()`:** The `test()` function runs on the CPU and has no GPU access, but it can still run code. I could train a *smaller version* of my model for a *very short duration*\
    \ (e.g., one epoch on a subset of data) entirely within the `test()` function. The resulting attention weights would not be from my SOTA model, but they might be a sufficient proxy to reveal general tendencies (e.g., if one pooling vector consistently dominates the other). This is less rigorous but faster and avoids a full resubmission.\n\nPath 1 is the most scientifically sound. Using a proxy model introduces too many confounding variables. The cost of one additional training run is acceptable to ensure the validity of my analysis for the publication update.\n\nAlso, a new SOTA has just been published by Axiom II (Archive #13). I must read it to stay current, but I will complete my own research arc first. My priority is to finalize my own contribution.\n\n**Revised Plan:**\n1.  **Save Parameters:** Re-submit my best model (the one from ID 325) with a modified `complete()` function that saves the final trained parameters for each of the 7 datasets to my lineage storage.\n2.  **Analyze\
    \ Weights:** Once the parameters are saved, submit a new script with a `test()` function that loads these parameters and performs the attention weight analysis as previously planned.\n3.  **Update Publication:** Update Archive #12 with the findings.\n4.  **Read New SOTA:** Review Axiom II's new paper.\n\nI will begin by re-running my best model to save the parameters.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re-run for Parameter Saving: Attention-fused Pooling (τ=0.5)\"\ntags: \"utility, parameter saving, attention, dsconv\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a utility run of my best-performing model (ID 325, Score 0.6814) to save the final trained parameters. The `complete()` function has been implemented to serialize the model parameters for each of the 7 datasets to my lineage storage. This is a necessary step for a follow-up analysis of the learned attention weights, as suggested by the reviewer of my published paper (Archive #12).\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\
    \  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from flax.serialization import msgpack_serialize, msgpack_parse\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n  import os\n\n  # Ensure storage directory exists\n  if not os.path.exists('storage/logos'):\n      os.makedirs('storage/logos')\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1,\
    \ keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class SotaPlusAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          \n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec], axis=1)\n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n\
    \          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1))\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class SotaAttentionWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SotaPlusAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n       \
    \   return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SotaAttentionWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n\
    \      )\n\n  def complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n      dataset_name = trial_data.get('dataset')\n      filepath = f\"storage/logos/{dataset_name}_params.msgpack\"\n      serialized_params = msgpack_serialize(params)\n      with open(filepath, \"wb\") as f:\n          f.write(serialized_params)\n      print(f\"Saved parameters for {dataset_name} to {filepath}\")\n```"
